<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Two servers, deliberately different transports:

    LAN dev server   http://192.168.x.x:8765   plain HTTP  (a fixed API token guards it)
    EC2 box          https://<elastic-ip>:8443  mutual TLS (client cert + token)

  Cleartext is permitted below because the LAN case needs it and Android's network security
  config CANNOT express a CIDR range (it can scope cleartext only by hostname), and the LAN IP
  is numeric and changes. So we cannot say "cleartext for 192.168.0.0/16 only".

  == What actually protects the scary secret ==
  The broker PASSWORD is never sent over cleartext: TradingViewModel.loginWith() BLOCKS (not just
  warns) when passwordInClear(baseUrl) is true, so adding an account requires https (EC2) or
  loopback. What crosses the LAN in the clear is the API token (the user's accepted risk for a
  fixed-token dev server) plus market data and trade commands, not a broker credential.

  The EC2 path does NOT rely on this file: OkHttp is configured with a custom SSLSocketFactory +
  X509TrustManager (trusting our private CA only) and a client certificate, which OVERRIDES the
  trust-anchors here for those calls. See net/Tls.kt / data/CertStore.kt.

  If this app ever drops the LAN case and talks only to the EC2 box, delete
  cleartextTrafficPermitted below and cleartext is refused platform-wide.

  (Tailscale/WireGuard was an earlier design and has been removed; mutual TLS replaced it.)
-->
<network-security-config>

    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>

</network-security-config>
